---
description: Prefer function expressions over function declarations in all new JS/TS.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Function expressions, not declarations

Always write functions as expressions assigned to `const`. Do not use `function` declarations.

Prefer arrow expressions. Use `const name = function () {}` only when you need a non-arrow `this` or `arguments`.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
function loadStories() {
  return stories;
}

export default function KrakowMap() {
  return null;
}

// ✅ GOOD
const loadStories = () => {
  return stories;
};

const KrakowMap = () => {
  return null;
};

export default KrakowMap;
```

Allowed exceptions: class methods, object literal methods, and generators (`function*`).
